1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A socket names one endpoint: an address and a port. The port is the part the program chooses.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
A connection is named by four numbers: source address, source port, destination address, destination port.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The port space is 16 bits: 1024 well-known, 48128 registered, 16384 dynamic, summing to 65536.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
So the unit of a socket question is a pair, and the unit of a connection question is a four-tuple.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
The server calls socket, then bind, then listen. Only after listen can a client reach it.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The client calls socket and connect. connect binds an ephemeral port implicitly and sends the SYN.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
accept takes one completed connection off the queue and returns a descriptor for it. It does not answer the SYN.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Then read and write on the connected descriptor, and close it. The listening socket closes separately.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
After one client connects, the server holds two sockets on the same local port 443.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Every accept returns a fresh descriptor. Descriptors 3, 4, 5 and so on, one per connection.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
So a server with N connections holds 1 + 2N sockets in the system: one listening, N at the server, N at the clients.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
A server therefore scales by descriptors and memory, not by port numbers.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
connect sends a SYN and waits. Half a round trip later the server answers SYN-ACK.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
connect returns when the SYN-ACK arrives, at one round trip. The final ACK is still in flight.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
accept returns at one and a half round trips, when the ACK arrives and the connection joins the queue.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The passive states are LISTEN, then SYN-RECEIVED, then ESTABLISHED. The client goes SYN-SENT, then ESTABLISHED.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
listen(fd, n) says how many completed connections may wait for accept: the backlog is a queue length.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Six arrivals, a queue of two, and two accepts: four get queued, two are dropped.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The invariant: every arrival is either queued or dropped, and every queued one is accepted or still waiting.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
A full queue is what a SYN flood exploits, and it is why a server separates the half-open and completed queues.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Fix the server endpoint and the only thing that can vary is the client side.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
With the Linux default range, one client address can hold 28232 connections to one server endpoint.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Across all clients the space is 2^48 four-tuples: 2^32 addresses times 2^16 ports on each side.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
So the server side is bounded by descriptors, and the client side by ports. They are different ceilings.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
The side that sends the first FIN enters TIME_WAIT. The side that receives it goes to CLOSE-WAIT.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
It waits so that a lost final ACK can be answered, and so that old duplicates die before the tuple is reused.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The specification says two times the maximum segment lifetime: 2 x 120 = 240 seconds. Linux uses 60.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
TIME_WAIT holds a four-tuple; CLOSE_WAIT holds a descriptor. Only the first blocks a new connection.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Ports in use equals the connection rate times the seconds each port is held.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
28232 ports held for 60 seconds gives 470 new connections per second. 471 exceeds the range.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
The failure is EADDRNOTAVAIL from connect, not a refusal from the server.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The fixes change the rate or the hold: reuse connections, or widen the range.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A UDP socket is one endpoint and nothing else: no listen, no accept, no connection.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
A UDP socket is demultiplexed by its local port alone, so two clients share one receiving socket.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
connect on a UDP socket only records a default peer; it sends nothing and completes no handshake.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
With no connection there is no TIME_WAIT, so UDP cannot exhaust ports the way TCP does.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Four steps: name the side, name the quantity, apply the invariant, then check the unit.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
The invariants: 1 + 2N sockets, 2^48 tuples, 28232 ports, 1 and 1.5 round trips.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Trap one: accept returns the same socket. Trap two: a new port per connection. Trap three: the server enters TIME_WAIT.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Trap four: the backlog is a client count. Trap five: TIME_WAIT is always harmful. Write the unit, then compute.

